! THE INTERVAL TIMER AND THE TIME CLOCK ACTUALLY STEP.
!
! Hand-written to the shape of jb_ax5b routine 1258 (whose two halves
! are transcribed as encoding oracles in programs/ax5b_1258_test1.strap
! and programs/ax5b_1258_test2.strap), NOT a transcription.  Every
! assertion below is one the deck also makes; the deck's own blocks
! cannot be run yet because each ends in the SIC/SERS error harness and
! the %IQSZ print macros, none of which this model has.  What is
! transcribed is the CONTRACT, not the code.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------
! THE PRIMARY SOURCE.  Reference Manual printed p.34 (PDF 37), read
! first-hand at 200 dpi (the page has no text layer), "Interval Timer":
!
!   "The value of the interval timer reading is stored in core storage
!    and occupies bit positions 0 through 18 of location 1.  It is
!    continually stepped down by pulses originating from a stable
!    oscillator.  Each time the oscillator delivers a pulse, the value
!    of the timer is read out, decremented by one, and returned to core
!    storage.  The oscillator operates at 1,024 (2^10) cycles per
!    second, or a pulse about every millisecond.  Bits 0-8 show time in
!    seconds.  A full cycle is about 8 1/2 minutes."
!
!   "The timer runs whenever the machine is operating, which includes
!    also the execution of BRANCH ENABLED AND WAIT.  Whenever it goes
!    from one to zero, it turns on the time signal indicator in the
!    indicator register.  The next oscillator pulse then sets the timer
!    to all ones unless the program in the meantime has changed its
!    contents.  The timer does not stop after reaching zero."
!
!   "The only store-type operations that may store in the left half of
!    location 1, and hence may be used to set the interval timer, are
!    STORE VALUE, STORE COUNT, STORE REFILL, and STORE VALUE IN
!    ADDRESS, and these operations can be performed only when the
!    interruption system is disabled."
!
!   PROGRAMMING NOTE, same page: "Core storage provides valid and
!    usable storage locations for each bit position of location 1,
!    including bit positions 19 through 27 which are unused and appear
!    between the interval timer and the time clock. ... Specifically,
!    when STORE VALUE is used to reset the interval timer and bits 19
!    through 24 of the value placed in the interval timer contain any
!    one-bits, these bits are set into the corresponding bit positions
!    of location 1, and remain there until THE FIRST TIME THE INTERVAL
!    TIMER IS STEPPED DOWN."
!
! and printed p.35 (PDF 38), "Time Clock":
!
!   "This clock consists of a number 36 bits long which is continually
!    stepped up by pulses originating from the same 1024 cycle per
!    second oscillator which controls the updating of the interval
!    timer.  The two clocks are updated consecutively in the same time
!    interval.  The leftmost 26 bits of the time clock measure time in
!    seconds.  A full cycle is about 777 days."
!
!   "The value of the clock occupies bits 28-63 of location 1.  Each
!    time the oscillator delivers a pulse it is read out, incremented
!    by one, using the index adder, and returned to core storage."
!
! TIME CLOCK versus INTERVAL TIMER, since the deck's own title ("CHK
! TIME CLOCK AND INTERVAL TIMER") names both and conflating them is the
! kind of error this project exists to catch:
!
!   interval timer  bits 0-18 of word 1   19 bits   counts DOWN
!                   settable (SV/SC/SR/SVA, disabled only)
!                   raises TS on the 1 -> 0 transition
!                   full cycle 2^19 ticks = about 8 1/2 minutes
!   time clock      bits 28-63 of word 1  36 bits   counts UP
!                   NOT settable at all ("cannot be altered under
!                   program control" -- p.35)
!                   raises NOTHING ("no indication is given when the
!                   clock recycles to zero" -- p.35)
!                   full cycle 2^36 ticks = about 777 days
!
!   One oscillator, two counters, opposite directions, one word.
!
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------
! THE RACE THIS PROGRAM DOES NOT HAVE, and why it is built the way it
! is.  The obvious shape -- set the timer, SNAPSHOT word 1, spin until
! it differs from the snapshot -- is wrong here, because a pulse can
! land between the SV and the snapshot and the snapshot then holds a
! value the test did not choose.  Every block below instead spins until
! word 1 differs from THE CONSTANT IT WAS SET TO, and asserts that the
! first differing value is the stepped one.  A pulse landing before the
! first read is then harmless: the loop simply exits one iteration
! earlier with the same value.  The only assumption left is that one
! loop iteration is much shorter than one tick (about 62 clocks against
! 3255), so two steps can never be missed.
!
! WHY KV AND NOT A FULL-WORD COMPARE.  Reference Manual printed p.24:
! "COMPARE VALUE (KV): The address part, bits 0-24, of the half-word
! addressed by bits 0-18 of the effective address is compared with the
! address part, bits 0-24, of the index word specified by J."  Bits
! 0-24 are the interval timer (0-18) plus the unused bits (19-24) and
! EXCLUDE the time clock at 28-63 -- which is running, and would make
! any full-word compare of word 1 unequal every time.  This is why the
! deck reads word 1 as an index word and compares with KV, and why
! TEST 1 uses SR/SC to get at the clock: the fields of an index word
! are exactly the fields of location 1.
!
! WHY THE HALT IDIOM IS "B, $" AND NOT THE HOUSE "BEW, $".  BRANCH
! ENABLED AND WAIT enables the interruption system.  By the time this
! program finishes, TS is ON (block 4 put it there), and the model
! forces mask bits 0-19 to one, so TS is permanently masked on --
! enabling at the end would take a TS interruption into an interruption
! table this program does not have.  Every halt here is therefore an
! unconditional self-branch, which halts the model the same way (the
! core halts on "the instruction branched to its own address"), and
! WHICH self-branch is reached is the test's main observable: the
! PC= check in run_tests.sh pins the passing one, and each failure has
! its own address.
!
! WORST-CASE LENGTH, against gen_tb.py's 30000-step cap.  A PASSING run
! leaves each spin on the first pulse after it started, so it costs one
! tick per spin: five spins, about 5 x 3256 = 16300 clocks, plus the
! straight-line rows.  (MEASURED: see the run line in run_tests.sh.)
! The counts are sized so that the spins cannot time out BEFORE a tick
! arrives -- the cheapest iteration here is block 4's two instructions,
! about 28 clocks, so 300 of them span 8400 -- and so that a FAILING
! run, which halts at the first block that fails, cannot reach the cap
! either: the worst case is four passing spins (13000) plus block 5's
! 150 iterations of ~78 clocks (11700), about 25000.
!
! WHAT THIS DOES AND DOES NOT SAY ABOUT THE RATE.  Nothing a program
! can do inside the model measures 0.3 us, so the exact figure -- 3255
! clocks per tick or 3256 -- rests on the arithmetic in
! StretchTiming.bsv (78125 model clocks = 24 ticks, exactly) and not on
! anything here.  But the HALT CYCLE COUNT is a rate bracket all the
! same, and a fairly tight one: every spin leaves on the first pulse
! after it started, so a passing run costs almost exactly five ticks.
! MEASURED: 16575 clocks, against 5 x 3256 = 16280 plus the
! straight-line rows.  Halve the divider and the five spins no longer
! fit under gen_tb.py's 30000-step cap (5 x 6510 = 32550 -- ARITHMETIC,
! not measured; it is the only unmeasured claim in this header).
! Multiplying the rate by 780 IS measured -- injection 7 below -- and
! the spins then exit after 414 clocks with the wrong value in R1.  So the CYCLES<= bound and the step cap
! together bracket the rate to within about a factor of two.  What is
! tested exactly is everything downstream of the pulse: direction,
! amount, the reload, the unused bits, TS's edge, and the clock.

! ---------------------------------------------------------------------
! PROVEN DISCRIMINATING.  Eight injections, each one WHOLE LINE of
! bsv/StretchStorage.bsv or bsv/StretchTiming.bsv, each applied by a
! script that aborts unless the anchor line is unique AND unless
! exactly one line changed, and each reverted the same way with its own
! one-line diff printed.  These are the OBSERVED results, not the
! predicted ones.  The clean run is: all three word checks PASS,
! cycles 16575, halt PC 5179 (label PASS).
!
!  # | the line changed                     | OBSERVED
! ---+--------------------------------------+-----------------------------
!  1 | if (oscPulse) -> if (False)          | halt F1 (5180); all three
!    |   the pulse does nothing             |   word checks read 0.  4 fail.
!  2 | t - 1 -> t + 1                       | halt PASS; R1 = 4, R2 = 1,
!    |   the timer counts UP                |   R3 = 4 (all <<45).  3 fail.
!    |                                      |   TS still fires: the edge
!    |                                      |   test reads the OLD value,
!    |                                      |   which is still 1.  So the
!    |                                      |   DIRECTION is caught by the
!    |                                      |   values, not by the halt.
!  3 | if (t == 1) -> if (t == 0)           | halt F3T (5183) at 9852
!    |   TS on the WRONG EDGE               |   clocks.  1 fail.  THE ONE
!    |                                      |   docs/NEXT.md 5 predicted
!    |                                      |   would wreck the suite.
!  4 | 9'b0 -> w1[44:36]                    | halt PASS; EXACTLY ONE check
!    |   unused bits 19-27 survive the step |   fails, R3 = 0x5f0000000000
!    |                                      |   -- timer still 2, bits
!    |                                      |   19-23 still 11111.
!  5 | c + 1 -> c                           | halt F5 (5185) at 25554
!    |   the time clock is frozen           |   clocks.  2 fail (the spin
!    |                                      |   runs to its 150-iteration
!    |                                      |   bound; the CYCLES bound
!    |                                      |   catches that too).
!  6 | c + 1 -> c + 2                       | halt F6 (5186).  1 fail.
!    |   the clock steps by two             |
!  7 | oscPulseDenom 78125 -> 100           | halt F3T (5183) at 414
!    |   the oscillator 780x too fast       |   clocks.  4 fail.  So the
!    |                                      |   RATE is bracketed from
!    |                                      |   above by this program, and
!    |                                      |   from below by the 30000
!    |                                      |   step cap.
!  8 | {lh, w1[35:0]} -> {lh, 36'b0}        | halt F7 (5187).  1 fail.
!    |   a store into word 1 reaches the    |   Block 6 exists for this
!    |   time clock (p.35 suppression gone) |   one and nothing else
!    |                                      |   catches it.
!
! ONE INJECTION WAS DROPPED, deliberately and for cost: "if (tsFired)
! ind[tsIndex] = 1" -> "if (False)", i.e. the pulse happens but never
! reaches the indicator register.  Its observable is halt F4, which
! injection 1 already produces by a different route, and each injection
! costs a full mkStretchCore recompile.  Recorded here rather than left
! unmentioned.
!
! NO NULLS.  Every injection above moved something.  Injection 8 WOULD
! have been a null before block 6 was written: with only blocks 1-5 the
! store that clobbers the clock happens in blocks 1-4, and block 5's
! snapshot-then-spin is taken afterwards, so a clobbered clock simply
! restarts from zero and still steps by one.
! ---------------------------------------------------------------------

        SLC, (8)5000.0

START   BD,  $+.32                  ! disable.  p.34: SV into the left half
                                    ! of word 1 "can be performed only when
                                    ! the interruption system is disabled";
                                    ! enabled it raises DS and is suppressed.
                                    ! (Reset state is already disabled; this
                                    ! says so on purpose, as routine 1260
                                    ! does before every provoked store.)

! ---- 1.  IT STEPS, AND IT STEPS DOWN BY EXACTLY ONE ----------------
! Deck witness: 1258 TEST 2 blocks I587/I587A/I587B ("-INTERVAL TIMER
! FAILS TO STEP IN / -ONE MILLISEC") and I588 ("-INTERVAL TIMER FAILS
! TO STEP / -FROM 000000.4 TO 000000.0").
        LX,  $X1, K3                ! value 3 in bits 0-18
        SV,  $X1, 1.0               ! interval timer := 3
        LCI, $X5, 200
L1      LX,  $X4, 1.0               ! word 1 as ONE index word
        KV,  $X4, K3                ! bits 0-24 only: the clock is 28-63
        BZXE, D1                    ! XE off => they differ => it stepped
        CB,  $X5, L1
        B,   F1                     ! never stepped
D1      SV,  $X4, R1.0              ! bits 0-24 of the first stepped value
                                    ! expected 2: down by one, no wrap

! ---- 2.  THE RELOAD: 0 -> ALL ONES ---------------------------------
! Deck witness: 1258 TEST 2 block I589/I5810, "-INTERVAL TIMER FAILS TO
! STEP / -FROM 000000.0 TO 777777.40", against I58K1 = all ones in bits
! 0-18 and nothing else.
!
! p.34 says this as a RELOAD -- "the next oscillator pulse then sets
! the timer to all ones" -- and the model implements it as a plain
! decrement modulo 2^19, which produces the same bits: 0 - 1 = all
! ones.  The two descriptions coincide, including the "unless the
! program in the meantime has changed its contents" clause, because a
! program change is just a different value to decrement.  Stated here
! because the coincidence is the whole reason the faithful rule is
! cheap; see StretchStorage.bsv for the version of this note that
! matters to the implementation.
        LX,  $X1, K0
        SV,  $X1, 1.0               ! interval timer := 0
        LCI, $X5, 200
L2      LX,  $X4, 1.0
        KV,  $X4, K0
        BZXE, D2
        CB,  $X5, L2
        B,   F2                     ! did not step off zero
D2      SV,  $X4, R2.0              ! expected 777777.40: all ones, 0-18

! ---- 3.  THE UNUSED BITS 19-23 ARE CLEARED BY THE FIRST STEP -------
! Deck witness: 1258 TEST 2 block I5811/I5812, timer := I58K2
! ("1.77 00": bit 17 set AND bits 18-23 all ones), expecting BIT17
! ("1.00 00") after the step, and widening its own error text from
! "0-18." to "0-23." exactly here.  K3F below IS I58K2's printed value.
!
! p.34's programming note covers bits 19 THROUGH 24; XW's value field
! is reached as "word.bit" and that notation stops at bit 23, so this
! block tests 19-23, the same five the deck tests.  Bit 24 is left
! untested -- see the header of verify_ax5b_1258_test2_listing.py.
        LX,  $X1, K3F               ! bits 0-18 = 3, bits 19-23 = 11111
        SV,  $X1, 1.0
        LCI, $X5, 200
L3      LX,  $X4, 1.0
        KV,  $X4, K3F
        BZXE, D3
        CB,  $X5, L3
        B,   F3
D3      SV,  $X4, R3.0              ! expected 2 with 19-23 CLEAR, i.e.
                                    ! bit 17 alone -- the deck's BIT17

! ---- 3a.  TS IS STILL OFF -------------------------------------------
! THE DISCRIMINATOR FOR THE RELOAD RULE'S POLARITY, and the reason this
! one instruction is here rather than in block 4.  p.34 fires TS on the
! 1 -> 0 TRANSITION: "whenever it goes from one to zero".  The obvious
! wrong implementation tests the NEW value instead -- "decrement; if now
! zero, set TS" -- and block 4 cannot tell the two apart, because
! stepping 1 -> 0 satisfies both.  Block 2 can: it stepped 0 -> all
! ones, where the wrong rule (reading the OLD value as zero, or the new
! value after a 1->0 that never happened) fires and the right one does
! not.  Nothing in blocks 1-3 passes through one, so TS must be off.
!
! This matters more than it looks.  docs/NEXT.md 5 records that the
! model forces mask bits 0-19 to one, so TS is ALWAYS masked on and
! outranks DS and every PG indicator in the leftmost-wins scan: a timer
! that sets TS on the wrong edge would wrap every 3255 clocks and give
! every enabled program a TS interruption it never took before.
        BTS, F3T                    ! TS on already => wrong edge

! ---- 4.  TS FIRES ON THE 1 -> 0 TRANSITION --------------------------
! Deck witness: jb_ax5b routine 1260's I6071 block
! (programs/ax5b_16071_ts.strap), which is this block almost
! instruction for instruction -- "LX,$X4,BIT18 / SV,$X4,1.0 /
! LCI,$X4,512 / CB,$X4,$ / BTS,I6088", error text "UNABLE TO TURN TS
! ON."  That block's header says it is NOT run on the model because
! "this model's interval timer does not tick"; this block is the
! runnable statement of the same assertion.
        LX,  $X1, K1                ! the value ONE
        SV,  $X1, 1.0               ! interval timer := 1
        LCI, $X5, 300
L4      BTS, D4                     ! time signal on?
        CB,  $X5, L4
        B,   F4                     ! "UNABLE TO TURN TS ON."

! ---- 5.  THE TIME CLOCK STEPS UP BY EXACTLY ONE ---------------------
! Deck witness: 1258 TEST 1 blocks I581/I582 ("-TIME CLOCK FAILS TO
! STEP IN 1 MSEC.") and I583/I584 ("-BITS 46-63 ARE NOT CORRECT AFTER
! / -TIME CLOCK STEPPED.").  Same instructions, same registers' roles.
!
! Reference Manual printed p.24: "STORE REFILL (SR): The refill field,
! bits 46-63, of the index word specified by J replaces bits 0-17 of
! the half word specified by bits 0-18 of the effective address.  Bits
! 18-24 of the half word are set to zero."  Bits 46-63 of word 1 are
! the time clock's LOW 18 bits, so SR moves them where KV (bits 0-24)
! can see them, with 18-24 cleared so the compare is exact.
!
! "$X2" as an ADDRESS is storage word 18 -- index register 2 -- which
! is how the deck gets a scratch index word to SR into.  Reference
! Card, SPECIAL STORAGE ASSIGNMENT: index registers are words 16-31.
!
! THE CARRY IS NOT TESTED, and that is the deck's own limitation, not
! an oversight here: V+I adds into bits 0-17 modulo 2^18, so on the one
! tick in 2^18 where the clock's low half wraps, old+1 is zero while
! the new low half is also zero -- the compare still holds -- but the
! carry into bits 28-45 is never checked.  1258 TEST 1 has the sharper
! version of this defect: it propagates the carry with C+I into the
! COUNT field while its KV reads the VALUE field, so the carry lands
! where the comparison cannot see it.  See
! verify_ax5b_1258_test1_listing.py's "THE CARRY PATH DOES NOT REACH
! THE FIELD THAT IS COMPARED."
D4      LX,  $X0, 1.0
        SR,  $X0, $X2               ! clock bits 46-63 -> X2's value, 0-17
        LCI, $X5, 150
L5      LX,  $X0, 1.0
        SR,  $X0, $X3               ! the current low half
        KV,  $X3, $X2
        BZXE, D5                    ! differ => the clock stepped
        CB,  $X5, L5
        B,   F5                     ! "-TIME CLOCK FAILS TO STEP IN 1 MSEC."
D5      V+I, $X2, 1.0               ! old + 1 ...
        KV,  $X2, $X3               ! ... must be the new value: UP by one
        BZXE, F6                    ! stepped, but not up by exactly one

! ---- 6.  A STORE INTO WORD 1 DOES NOT DISTURB THE TIME CLOCK -------
! Reference Manual printed p.35: of the time clock, "its setting,
! however, cannot be altered under program control.  Any attempt to
! store into the right half of location 1 is suppressed, and indicator
! AD, address invalid, is turned on."  SV writes bits 0-24 -- the
! timer and the unused bits -- and bits 28-63 must come through
! untouched.  This is the block that would catch a writeWord which
! took the whole word from the instruction: the clock would be reset
! to whatever the index word's count and refill fields hold (zero),
! which no number of pulses between the two reads can explain.
!
! THE AD INDICATOR IS NOT TESTED, because it is not implemented: the
! suppression is modelled, the alarm is not.  See StretchStorage.bsv's
! writeWord and bsv/README.md "Scope".
!
! The compare accepts TWO answers -- unchanged, or up by exactly one --
! because a pulse may legitimately land between the two reads.  It
! rejects everything else.
        LX,  $X0, 1.0
        SR,  $X0, $X2               ! the clock's low half before
        LX,  $X1, K3
        SV,  $X1, 1.0               ! a store into word 1's LEFT half
        LX,  $X0, 1.0
        SR,  $X0, $X3               ! ... and after
        KV,  $X3, $X2
        BXE, PASS                   ! unchanged
        V+I, $X2, 1.0
        KV,  $X2, $X3
        BXE, PASS                   ! or one pulse landed between them
        B,   F7                     ! the store reached the time clock

PASS    B,   $

! ---- the failure halts, each at its own address ---------------------
F1      B,   $                      ! the interval timer never stepped
F2      B,   $                      ! it did not step off zero (no reload)
F3      B,   $                      ! bits 19-23 did not clear
F3T     B,   $                      ! TS fired on the wrong edge
F4      B,   $                      ! TS never came on
F5      B,   $                      ! the time clock never stepped
F6      B,   $                      ! the time clock did not step UP by one
F7      B,   $                      ! a store into word 1 reached the clock

! ---- constants and results ------------------------------------------
! The K constants are the values the timer is SET to and the values the
! spins compare against; their bit patterns are the deck's own.
        SLC, (8)5100.0
K0      XW,  0, 0, 0                ! all of bits 0-24 zero
K1      XW,  (8).40, 0, 0           ! bit 18 alone = the value ONE
                                    ! (this is the deck's BIT18, p.114)
K3      XW,  (8)1.40, 0, 0          ! bits 17 and 18 = the value THREE
K3F     XW,  (8)1.77, 0, 0          ! the deck's I58K2: bits 0-18 = 3 and
                                    ! bits 19-23 = 11111
R1      DD,  0                      ! first stepped value, block 1
R2      DD,  0                      ! first stepped value, block 2
R3      DD,  0                      ! first stepped value, block 3

        END, START
